WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.540
If someone steals our encrypted data,
can they read it?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.330
Usually not from a disk.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.400
Usually yes from a login.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.450
Four questions decide which.

00:00:10.883 --> 00:00:13.553
First, locked on the disk, open at login.

00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.983
A stolen disk or backup
yields nothing readable.

00:00:17.358 --> 00:00:21.948
A phished account or a leaked access
key reads everything in plain text.

00:00:22.268 --> 00:00:23.338
"Encrypted at rest"

00:00:23.788 --> 00:00:28.838
in a questionnaire answers the stolen disk
question, not the stolen login question.

00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:32.653
Second, whoever holds
the key holds the data.

00:00:32.918 --> 00:00:37.228
A key stored next to the data is a
safe with the key taped to the door.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.113
Cloud providers manage keys by default,

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:44.433
so anyone with enough admin access
in our account can use them.

00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:48.458
For the most sensitive data,
consider a key we manage ourselves,

00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:51.618
with different people
managing keys and data.

00:00:51.878 --> 00:00:54.388
Lose the key, and we lose the data too.

00:00:55.417 --> 00:00:57.407
Third, the copy nobody encrypted.

00:00:57.927 --> 00:00:59.517
The database is encrypted.

00:01:00.007 --> 00:01:02.477
The nightly export, the USB stick,

00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:07.017
the old laptop and the test system
loaded with real data may not be.

00:01:07.257 --> 00:01:09.127
One readable copy is enough.

00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:11.873
Fourth, the quantum question.

00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:16.753
Stored data under AES-256
is not the weak spot.

00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:20.028
A large quantum computer would
break public-key encryption,

00:01:20.558 --> 00:01:22.908
such as RSA and ECC.

00:01:23.343 --> 00:01:25.383
That matters for recorded network traffic,

00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:28.593
and for archives
encrypted to a public key.

00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:30.827
What good looks like.

00:01:31.112 --> 00:01:32.382
Access comes first:

00:01:33.132 --> 00:01:36.782
multi-factor login, least privilege,
and alerts on bulk reading.

00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:40.627
Keys are kept apart from data,
and every use is logged.

00:01:40.927 --> 00:01:43.297
Coverage is checked for every location.

00:01:43.662 --> 00:01:46.742
DORA's technical standard,
Articles 6 and 7,

00:01:46.742 --> 00:01:52.112
requires an encryption policy and key
management over the whole key lifecycle.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.040
A suggested first step.

00:01:55.325 --> 00:01:56.655
Ask three questions.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.840
Who can read it while the system runs?

00:01:59.215 --> 00:02:01.325
Where are the keys, and who can use them?

00:02:01.555 --> 00:02:03.165
Which copies are not encrypted?

00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:07.133
Is it encrypted is the wrong question.

00:02:07.423 --> 00:02:08.833
Ask who can read it.

